    "done_when": "Create the production fixed-point solve owner that the plan's design names (`solve.fixed_point`): `nova/equilibrium/solve.py`, a narrow chord-Newton solver over a pure map, batched over states. This moves the settled method out of the prototypes. It does not wire the forward route yet. The legacy `nova/equilibrium/fixed_point.py` stays until the §6 retirement node deletes it.\n\nThe settled method, on main:\n- **Runtime-argument batched chord.** In `scripts/prototypes/batched_factor/measure.py`, the state, the carrier operands and the factor are all arguments. Jacobian columns come from a vmapped `jax.jvp`, and the factor from `jax.scipy.linalg.lu_factor`/`lu_solve`. Two chord corrections reuse the predecessor factor. Result (docs/research/batched-factor-size.html): width-128 limited 289 cells, 1.36 MB executable, 33 s compile, 0.279 ms per state, 128/128 converged at 1e-12.\n- **Cold solve.** `scripts/prototypes/fast_map_production/solver.py` is the damped Newton with line search behind the converged 282-cell diverted solve: 1.25e-13 in five corrections.\n\nNarrow interface, and nothing else public:\n- a frozen result type, carrying `state`, `residual` (scaled infinity norm), `converged`, `corrections` and `factor`;\n- `factor(residual_fn, state, operands)`: build the dense Jacobian of `residual_fn(state, operands)` by vmapped jvp over identity columns, then LU-factor it;\n- `fixed_point(residual_fn, state, operands, *, factor=None, tolerance=1e-12, max_corrections=…)`: cold, with a fresh factor and damped backtracking, or chord, reusing the given factor.\n\n`residual_fn` is a pure JAX function, and the state is a flat array. Add no policy objects, no receipts, no host loops inside the traced path, and no state-dependent closure constants.\n\nDone when all of the following hold:\n(1) The module and `tests/test_equilibrium_solve.py` exist. CPU tests on an analytic nonlinear map with a known fixed point show:\n- cold convergence to 1e-12 with quadratic residual reduction, with the correction count asserted;\n- chord reuse from a neighbouring state's factor converging in two corrections;\n- `jax.vmap` over 16 distinct states, all converged;\n- a compiled program taking state, operands and factor as arguments. Inspect the lowered HLO constants, as the prototype's `hlo_constants` does, and assert no constant scales with the state.\n(2) `scripts/prototypes/batched_factor/measure.py` uses `nova.equilibrium.solve` for factor and chord in place of its local copy, and deletes that copy. It reproduces the prototype's width-128 limited row on the H200 through the production function: the same converged count, and per-state wall within 10% of 0.279 ms. Run it as one sbatch job on betelgeuse with reservation gpu_0003_grpA and one GPU. The node may queue for a card, so declare the wait.\n(3) The manifest lists the call sites in `fixed_point.py`, `forward.py` and `reduced_newton.py` that will move to `solve.fixed_point` at the §6 cutover. Do not edit them.\n\nRun conditions: H200 rows run as one job through RECKON_ALLOW_NESTED_LAUNCH=1 scripts/h200_test_lane/run.sh --log (an absolute path in the run directory) --wait -- (targets), or one sbatch job on --partition=betelgeuse --reservation=gpu_0003_grpA --gres=gpu:1 with rungs as rows inside the allocation (never an array), --time sized to finish, XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=false and TMPDIR=/tmp in the submit environment and in every child; submit for real and read squeue a minute later rather than trusting sbatch --test-only. On a compute node run /home/ITER/mcintos/Code/nova/.venv/bin/python directly with PYTHONPATH set to the run worktree and never invoke uv; every login-node uv command carries --no-sync and nothing runs uv sync. configure_dtypes() before any array and jax_enable_x64 asserted True. Gates pass by delta against orientation_base_sha in per-module fresh processes, both arms running the same test files with the repository root conftest, baseline_suite and after_suite as single-line JSON whose log_path names one log file and whose command is literally runnable. Any wait is declared on the job that finishes last as wait_probe [\"sacct\",\"-n\",\"-X\",\"-P\",\"-j\",\"(job id)\",\"-o\",\"State\"] and wait_terminal [\"COMPLETED\",\"FAILED\",\"CANCELLED\",\"TIMEOUT\",\"OUT_OF_MEMORY\",\"NODE_FAIL\"] as JSON lists, and names every job it depends on in wait_condition. The certified 550, 2,000 and 5,000-cell machines and exteriors are cached under /home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/runs/r-20261009T181553786165-proto-support/ordered/rows/cache and may be read in place, never written. Run filepath relativize over any evidence fragment before committing. Commit with conventional subjects and bodies at every step and write the manifest and commit before every job wait; the manifest commits line lists every commit; status is complete, blocked or failed, which the worker sets itself. No plan labels in source names or comments. Inside every job payload, make one directory with mktemp -d /tmp/$USER-$SLURM_JOB_ID-XXXX, point TMPDIR at it, and remove it on exit with trap 'rm -rf' (keep TMPDIR=/tmp in the submit environment so slurmstepd can start). Never leave extracted base trees or caches in /tmp after a node ends; anything a later reader needs goes under the run directory on GPFS.\n\nImport provenance: print nova.__file__ in every log header. On the fleet allocation (SLURM_JOB_ID set), run CPU work in place. The time budget is per working turn, not a deadline: park on H200 jobs with declared waits and continue across turns; this node is sized at about 8 worker-hours, and an unmeasured item in your own scope is remaining work, not a blocker. Never edit a script a submitted job will run; submit a per-job copy. Size every job memory from a measured row. The manifest commits line lists every commit (git rev-list), orientation_base_sha is copied from git rev-parse output, and status is complete when the record is delivered even if the target is not met (then give the nearest measured figure and what dominates it).",
